      "Language": "pol",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "Changes in gastric acidity induced by Helicobacter pylori (Hp) infection, may influence intensity of symptoms in patients diagnosed because of chest pain. The aim of this study was to compare the results of 24-hours gastric pH-metry in patients suffering from atypical chest pain infected and not-infected by Hp."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "In 99 men diagnosed because of atypical chest pain performed were: interview, physical examination, gastroduodenoscopy with musoca biopsy from gastric corpus and antrum, as well as 24-hour gastric pH-metry. Hp infection was diagnosed on the basis of positive urease test or/and histologic examination."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "78% of subjects were infected by Hp. Hp positive patients had lower total and night-time percentage of monitoring time with gastric pH<4 and greater with pH > 6. Patients with isolated antral Hp colonization and subjects with pangastritis didn't differ in respect to gastric pH-metry parameters values. However in patients, with Hp colonization only in gastric corpus the time with gastric pH < 4 was shorter and with pH > 6 longer than in other groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Hp-positive patients with atypical chest pain had lower gastric acidity than Hp-negative subjects. In patients with corporal Hp gastric mucosa colonization higher intra-gastric pH was observed than in the rest of patients."
        }
      ]},
